-- Reworded six of the 2026-10-20 dissociation articles so each one names
-- dissociation in its own text, which is what the library search reads. Generated by:
--   python3 tools/articles_sql.py <the six slugs>
-- An upsert on slug, so it refreshes the text and keeps every id.
insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('losing-chunks-of-time', 'Losing Chunks of Time', 'An hour is gone and you cannot say what you did with it. Why the mind drops stretches of time, how to tell the ordinary kind from the kind worth checking, and practical ways to stay with yourself.', 'It is twenty past three. The last thing you remember clearly is sitting down at half past one. You are in the same chair. Nothing bad has happened. But the two hours in between are not there, and no amount of searching for them brings anything back.

This is frightening in a particular way, because time is the thing we use to feel like one continuous person. When a piece of it goes missing, it can feel as though you went missing with it. You probably have not told anyone, in case it makes you sound unwell. It is worth knowing that this happens to a great many people, that it has understandable causes, and that it responds to help.

Why the mind drops time

Your sense of a continuous day is built, not recorded. Your brain lays down memory as you go, and it does that best when something has your attention and matters to you. When attention goes elsewhere for long enough, nothing gets written down, and later there is nothing to find.

Several ordinary things make this more likely. Exhaustion. Long stretches of repetitive work. Weeks of unrelenting stress. Grief. Very high anxiety, which uses up so much attention that little is left for the rest of the day.

There is also the protective kind, the one usually called dissociation. When something is too much to be fully present for, the mind steps back, and the hours around it do not get recorded. For people who lived through frightening or unsafe situations, especially when young, this was once a genuinely useful ability: a way of not being wholly there for something unbearable. The skill does not switch itself off when the danger ends. It stays available, and it can activate at times you would not choose, often when something in the present quietly resembles the past.

Some physical causes look exactly like this too. Sleep problems, certain medications, alcohol, low blood sugar, migraine, and some kinds of seizure can all take pieces out of a day. That is why the first step is a doctor rather than a search engine.

Telling the two kinds apart

The ordinary kind usually has a shape: a dull task, a long drive, a tired afternoon, and you were plainly in one place doing one thing the whole time.

Pay more attention if you find objects moved or work completed that you do not remember doing. If you come back somewhere other than where you started. If people describe conversations you have no memory of. If you find yourself dressed differently, or with injuries you cannot account for. If it is happening several times a week, or it is getting worse. Please take those to a doctor, not because they mean something terrible, but because they deserve a proper look rather than private worry.

Build an outside record

When your own memory of a stretch of time is unreliable, borrow a record from the outside. Keep a small notebook or a note on your phone and write one line every hour or two: the time, where you are, what you are doing. It takes five seconds and it does two useful things. It gives you something to read afterward instead of a blank. And the act of writing it pulls your attention back to the present for a moment, which makes the next gap less likely.

Put landmarks through the day

Gaps grow in long, featureless stretches. Breaking the day into pieces gives your memory something to hang on. An alarm every ninety minutes. Standing up and walking to the window between tasks. Drinking a glass of water. Stepping outside at the same two times each day. None of this is dramatic. All of it makes the day less like one undifferentiated block.

Come back through the senses

Thinking your way back does not work well, because thinking is the part that drifted. Sensation is more reliable and much faster. Cold water on your face or wrists. An ice cube in your hand. Feet pressed hard into the floor. Something sour or strongly minty. A rough stone or your keys in your pocket to rub between your fingers.

Then say where and when you are, out loud if you can: I am in my kitchen, it is Tuesday evening, it is October. It feels odd to do. It works, because it reinstates the exact thing the state has lost.

Track what comes before

Afterward, when thinking is available again, write down what was happening in the hour before. Over a few weeks, most people find a pattern: a certain person, a certain kind of pressure, a particular place, being very short of sleep, a specific time of year. A pattern turns something random into something you can see coming, which lets you use the tools early.

Be careful about the frightened story

The explanation people land on alone is usually the worst one available: that this means they are losing their mind, or that they are not a reliable person. Neither follows. A mind that steps back from too much is doing something protective, clumsily and at the wrong moment. It is not evidence of anything broken in your character.

If it gets dark

Losing pieces of yourself is genuinely distressing, and for some people it comes with a flat sense that nothing is real or that it would not matter what happened to them. If thoughts like that are around, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if you are losing time, so the physical causes can be ruled out, and ask about a therapist who works with trauma if the cause turns out to be something you lived through. There are people who understand this well, who will not be alarmed by it, and who can help it fade instead of becoming something you manage alone forever.', array['Trauma', 'Grounding']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T15:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-the-world-looks-like-a-film-set', 'When the World Looks Like a Film Set', 'Colors look flat, familiar rooms look staged, and nothing quite feels real. What that unreal quality is, why the fear of it keeps it going, and how to let it settle.', 'The kitchen you have stood in for nine years looks like a set built to resemble your kitchen. Colors seem flat, or too bright. Sounds arrive a half second late. The faces of people you love look like faces, correctly arranged, and somehow not quite theirs. Nothing has changed and everything looks slightly counterfeit.

People who have felt this often describe it as the strangest thing that has ever happened to them, and then do not mention it to anyone, because every way of saying it sounds alarming. It is more common than you would guess from how rarely it comes up, it is not a sign that you are losing your mind, and it does settle.

What this is

When the world looks unreal, that is usually derealization. When it is you who seems unreal, as though you were watching yourself from a few feet behind, that is depersonalization. They frequently come together, and both are forms of dissociation: a protective turning down of the volume when a system is overwhelmed. Some people say they dissociate; others only ever say the room went strange.

A useful way to picture it is a circuit breaker. When too much current comes through at once, the breaker trips to protect the wiring. What trips here is the sense of immediacy, the feeling of being right up against the world. The machinery still works perfectly. You can see, hear, think, drive, hold a conversation. What has gone is the sensation of nearness.

It shows up most after high and sustained stress, after a frightening event, during or after panic, when you are very short of sleep, during long periods of grief, and sometimes with certain substances or a new medication. Cannabis is a common trigger, and the state can outlast the use by a long way.

Why the fear keeps it going

Here is the part that changes things. The state itself is uncomfortable. What makes it persist is the alarm about it.

The sequence is reliable. You notice the world looks odd. You ask yourself what is wrong with me and start checking, repeatedly, whether things still look unreal. Checking is a form of intense self-focus, and intense self-focus is exactly what produces the unreal quality in the first place. Meanwhile the fear keeps your system on high alert, which is the condition that tripped the breaker to begin with. Worry about it, monitor it, test it, and you reliably feed it.

This is genuinely good news, because it means the thing you are most afraid of, that this is permanent, is also the thing most within your influence. Most people find the state loosens once the fear of it does.

Stop checking

Cut the monitoring, as best you can. Do not test whether your hands look like your hands. Do not peer at your reflection to see if your face looks right. Do not ask people repeatedly whether you seem normal. Every check is a small announcement to your nervous system that there is an emergency here worth watching.

When you notice yourself checking, name it plainly and turn your attention outward. That is the checking. The room is fine. Then look at something and do something.

Give it a dull explanation on purpose

Fear thrives on a dramatic story. Practice a boring one until it is the first thing that arrives.

This is my system turning down the volume because it is overloaded. It is unpleasant. It is not dangerous. It will pass, as it has before.

Say it flatly, the way you would read a label. You are not trying to convince yourself of something nice. You are replacing an emergency with a description.

Come back through your senses, then leave it alone

Sensation is more useful than thought here, because thought is the foggy part. Cold water on your face. Pressing your feet into the floor. Something rough between your fingers. A strong taste. Moving: walking, stairs, shaking out your hands.

Then, and this is the important half, go and do something ordinary and absorbing without waiting to feel normal first. Cook. Weed something. Fix a thing. Talk to someone about their day. Watch something. Feeling follows participation far more often than participation follows feeling, and waiting at the window for reality to come back is the one approach that never works.

Look after the ground conditions

This state gets dramatically more likely when you are running on four hours of sleep, skipping meals, drinking to get to sleep, or holding weeks of pressure in your shoulders. None of that is glamorous to address and all of it matters. If it began after cannabis or another substance, stopping is part of the answer, even if the effect has long outlasted the use.

Tell one person

Say it out loud to someone you trust, even clumsily. Things look unreal to me lately and it unnerves me. Describing it tends to shrink it, partly because the secrecy is its own kind of pressure, and partly because almost everyone you tell will say something unremarkable rather than something frightened.

When to get help

Please see your doctor, especially if this is new, if it came with headaches, fainting, or memory trouble, if it started with a medication, or if it has gone on for weeks, since there are physical causes worth ruling out. If the answer is stress, panic, or something you went through, a therapist can help, and this is well-trodden ground for anyone who treats anxiety. The world has not actually changed, and the nearness of it does come back.', array['Anxiety', 'Thinking Traps']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T15:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('going-blank-when-someone-asks-you-a-question', 'Going Blank When Someone Asks You a Question', 'Someone turns to you, and everything you knew leaves. Why the mind empties under the spotlight, what to say while you wait for it to come back, and how to make the blanks shorter.', 'Someone says your name and asks what you think. And there is nothing there. Not a wrong answer, not a half answer. A white wall. You can feel your face getting hot and your mouth saying um, and somewhere behind the wall you know perfectly well what you think about this, you just cannot get to it.

Then you say something flat and small, the conversation moves on, and twenty minutes later the whole fluent paragraph you meant to say arrives, complete, far too late.

Why the mind empties

Being asked a question in front of people is a social spotlight, and a body under a spotlight gets ready for threat. Blood and attention go to the parts that handle danger, and the parts you need, the ones that find words and search memory, get less. That is not a character flaw. It is a trade-off your body makes quickly and without consulting you.

Two things make it worse. The first is self-watching. The moment attention swings onto you, a lot of it goes to monitoring yourself: how your voice sounds, whether your face looks strange, how long the pause has lasted. Attention spent watching yourself is attention unavailable for thinking, and the blank deepens.

The second is the panic about the blank. The first second is a small stumble. By the fourth second, you are no longer looking for the answer, you are reacting to the silence, and the alarm from that keeps the door shut.

For some people, this has a longer history. If you grew up where being asked a question was often the opening move of something unpleasant, or where a wrong answer cost you, going still and empty under attention is a protection your system learned early and practiced well. That version feels less like nerves and more like a shutter coming down, and it is a small piece of dissociation: the mind stepping back from a moment it has decided is dangerous.

Have words ready for the blank itself

You cannot force the answer up, but you can make the silence survivable, which takes away the panic that extends it. Have two or three sentences so well practiced that they come out without any thinking.

Give me a second, I want to answer that properly.

Good question. Let me think about it for a moment.

Say more about what you are asking, so I answer the right thing.

I will have a better answer in five minutes than I will right now.

They all buy time, and the last two also hand the floor back for a moment. People read these as thoughtful. Nobody experiences them as failure, which is exactly what you cannot believe in the moment and is true anyway.

Buy time physically as well as verbally

Put your feet flat and press down. Let one breath out slowly. Pick up a glass of water and drink some. Look away from the faces for a second, at the table, at your notes. Looking away feels rude and is not; it reduces the spotlight enough for the language to come back. Sitting back in your chair rather than forward does something similar.

Stop performing while you wait

The blank gets longer while you are busy evaluating your own performance. If you catch yourself tracking how you look, move your attention outward on purpose: onto what the person actually asked, onto the object in front of you, onto the next thing you want to understand. You cannot think and audit yourself at the same time, and only one of those gets you an answer.

Prepare the shape, not the script

For meetings, interviews, classes, or appointments you know will put you on the spot, do not memorize sentences. Memorize two or three things you would want to say about the subject, in whatever words arrive. A memorized script is brittle: lose one line and the whole thing vanishes. A couple of remembered points are robust, and they are something to reach for when the shelf looks empty.

Write down what you wished you had said. Three times out of four it will come up again, and next time it will be findable.

Let yourself answer late

The answer that arrives twenty minutes later is not wasted. Send the message. Say it as you leave. Raise it at the start of the next meeting. I have been thinking about what you asked, and here is what I think. This quietly rebuilds the thing the blank damages, which is the belief that you have nothing to offer, and over time it lowers the stakes of the moment itself.

Expect the blanks to shorten rather than stop

Most people who work on this do not reach a point where nothing ever goes missing. They reach a point where the gap is two seconds instead of fifteen, where they have a line to fill it, and where it no longer ruins the afternoon. That is a real and sufficient outcome.

When to get help

If going blank is a regular part of your work or your studies, if the dread of being asked something is shaping where you go and what you take on, or if the blankness is more like a shutter coming down than ordinary nerves, talking it through with a therapist is worth your time. This is one of the better understood problems in the field and it responds well to practice with someone who knows how to build it. Your thinking is not the thing that is failing you here.', array['Social Anxiety', 'Communication']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T15:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('checking-out-in-the-middle-of-a-workday', 'Checking Out in the Middle of a Workday', 'You are at your desk, the screen is open, and you are not there. Why long stretches of the workday go missing, what it tends to mean, and how to get some of yourself back into the hours.', 'It is a quarter past four. You have been at your desk since nine. The document is open at the same place it was at two. You were not slacking off and you were not working. You were somewhere in between, staring at a screen with your hands near the keyboard, and the afternoon went past without you.

Then comes the second part, which is often worse: the feeling that you have wasted the day, followed by staying late to make up for hours you cannot account for.

What the checking out actually is

Attention is not willpower. It is a resource, and it runs down. When it runs far enough down, the mind stops engaging with what is in front of it and idles. You are awake, you look busy, and almost nothing is going in. At the far end this shades into dissociation, where you are plainly in the room and not in your own day.

Work makes this especially likely for a few plain reasons. The tasks are often repetitive enough to need no decisions. The environment barely changes from hour to hour. There is no natural pause between things. And if the work carries any dread, if opening that spreadsheet means facing a conversation you are avoiding, the mind will drift away from it the way you would step back from something hot.

Long-running stress is the other big driver. When you have been braced for weeks, going flat is what a system does to get through the day at a lower cost. That version is one of the more common faces of burnout, and it is often mistaken by the person having it for laziness, which it is not.

Tell the difference between tired and hollow

It is worth knowing which one you have, because the fixes are different.

Ordinary attention fatigue comes and goes with sleep, lifts on a weekend, and responds to a walk and a glass of water.

The hollow kind does not lift. You are checked out in week five of being checked out. You feel nothing much about work you used to care about, you get through the day on a kind of autopilot, and the weekend does not restore you. That one is not solved with a better calendar. It needs a change in the load, and usually some help.

Give the day a shape

Blank stretches grow in long featureless blocks. Break the block.

Work in defined pieces with real edges: forty or fifty minutes on one named thing, then stop and move. Stand up, walk to a window, get water, step outside. The point is less the rest than the boundary; a day with edges in it is far harder to lose.

Name the next action out loud or on paper before you start. Not finish the report. Write the first paragraph of the summary. Vague tasks are the easiest ones to drift away from, because there is no particular moment of being in them.

Make the beginning physical

Starting is where most checking out begins, so set the first thirty seconds deliberately. Close the other tabs. Put both feet on the floor. Say the task. One slow breath out. It sounds like very little and it is the difference between entering the work and hovering near it.

Change something in the room when you feel yourself go

The fastest route back is through the body, not through resolve. Cold water on your wrists or face. Open a window. Move to a different chair or a different room. Walk up a flight of stairs. Eat something. Change the light. Any strong, specific physical input will do more in sixty seconds than another ten minutes of staring will do in an hour.

Look at what you are drifting away from

Sometimes the fog is not general. It arrives exactly when a particular task does. If you track it for a week you may find your blank afternoons cluster around the same piece of work, the same client, or the same looming conversation.

That is useful, because then the problem is not your attention. The problem is the avoided thing, and a hard half hour spent on the avoided thing can hand back several foggy days.

Protect sleep and food before blaming yourself

A lot of what gets diagnosed as a focus problem is six hours of sleep and no lunch. You cannot concentrate your way out of a body that is short on both. If it has been a long stretch of late nights and skipped meals, start there before you try any technique, because the techniques work poorly on a tired system and well on a rested one.

Say something, if it has been going on

If weeks are disappearing, it is worth telling someone at work something true and limited: that the load has been heavy, that you are losing time, that you need to look at what comes first. You do not owe anyone an account of your inner life. You are allowed to renegotiate work that has become impossible to be present for.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if you are losing stretches of the day regularly, since sleep problems, thyroid trouble, low iron, medications, and other treatable things do exactly this. If it has lasted months, if the flatness follows you home, or if you have stopped caring about things that used to matter, a therapist can help you tell burnout from depression and work out what needs to change. Losing an afternoon is not a verdict on your character; usually it is a signal about your load.', array['Work', 'Burnout']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T15:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-hours-that-disappear-into-your-phone', 'The Hours That Disappear Into Your Phone', 'You meant to look at one thing and it is now ninety minutes later. Why a screen is such an easy place to go when you want to stop feeling, and how to come back without making a rule you cannot keep.', 'You pick up the phone to check one thing. The next time you look up it is ninety minutes later, you could not describe five things you saw, and you feel slightly worse than you did before without being able to say why.

Then the usual story arrives: no self-control, addicted to the screen, pathetic. That story is both unkind and not very accurate, and it does not help you stop.

What is really happening in that hour

A scrolling hour is mostly a way of not being somewhere. Not in a feeling, not in a task, not in a quiet room with your own thoughts. The screen is perfectly designed for it: endless, requiring nothing of you, new every few seconds. It is the easiest available exit, and it is right there in your hand.

This is why the hours vanish at such specific moments. After a hard conversation. When a task feels too big to start. At eleven at night, when the day''s thoughts finally have room to arrive. On the edge of a feeling you do not want to have. The drift is not random. It is almost always an exit from something.

And it works, which is why it keeps happening. For as long as you are scrolling you are not feeling much at all. That flat, slightly unreal quality afterward is the cost: you turned the volume down on everything for an hour, and it takes a while to come back up. It is a mild, everyday kind of dissociation, chosen rather than triggered.

Stop calling it a character problem

The character story has one effect, which is to add shame to an evening that was already a bit empty. Shame does not reduce the behavior, it increases the wish for an exit, which is the thing that puts the phone back in your hand.

Try a plainer description. I did not want to be in that feeling, so I left for an hour. It is accurate, it is not an accusation, and it points at something you can actually address.

Find out what you are leaving

For a few days, when you catch yourself coming back from a long scroll, write down one line: the time, and what was happening just before you picked up the phone.

Most people find three or four reliable doorways. Dread of a particular task. The hour before bed. Loneliness on a Sunday. Right after being criticized. Knowing your doorways is worth more than any app limit, because it tells you what the phone is doing for you, and nothing changes until you have something else that does part of the same job.

Put a small gap in the reach

You are not going to resolve your way out of a motion your hand makes without you. Make the motion take one more step.

Keep the phone in another room while you work. Charge it outside the bedroom. Take the busiest apps off the home screen so you have to search for them. Log out, so there is a password between you and the exit. None of this is willpower; it is friction, and friction is far more reliable than intention.

Have a second exit ready

Deciding to stop scrolling leaves a hole exactly where you least want one. Pick two or three small things that are available in the same thirty seconds the phone is: a kettle, a short walk around the block, one song with your eyes closed, stepping outside, five minutes of tidying, a message to an actual person. Write them on the back of a card and keep it where the phone usually sits.

Come back through the body, not the clock

When you surface from a long scroll feeling fuzzy and far away, do not immediately start assessing the evening. Stand up. Drink water. Put your face near cold air. Look out of a window at something far off, which also helps your eyes. Say the time and the day out loud. Then decide what the rest of the evening is for. The deciding goes much better from a body that has come back.

Protect the two edges of the day

The first and last thirty minutes shape the rest more than any other part. A phone in the first thirty minutes starts you reactive. A phone in the last thirty keeps you awake and leaves the quality of your sleep worse, which makes drifting easier tomorrow. If you change only one thing, change one of those two edges, and leave everything in the middle alone.

Aim for less, not none

Rules that forbid everything break in a week and leave you feeling worse. Pick something modest and specific: not in bed, or not in the first half hour, or no scrolling in the twenty minutes before a task I am dreading. One narrow boundary you actually keep will give you back more hours than a total ban you abandon.

When to get help

If the hours are swallowing most of your evenings, if you are losing sleep to them, or if you notice you are using the screen to get away from something heavy, like grief, a frightening memory, or a low mood that has not lifted in weeks, it is worth telling a doctor or a therapist. The scrolling is rarely the real problem; it is usually the thing standing where the real problem is, and that is much easier to look at with someone alongside you.', array['Habits', 'Self-Care']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T15:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-foggy-hour-after-you-come-back', 'The Foggy Hour After You Come Back', 'The spaced-out feeling has passed and you are wrung out, slow, and vaguely ashamed. What that after-stretch needs, what makes it worse, and how to use it instead of fighting it.', 'The far-away feeling has lifted. You are back in the room and you can think again. And you feel wrecked: heavy limbs, slow words, a headache coming, a strange flatness, and a low hum of embarrassment about whatever just happened.

Most of what gets written about spacing out is about the episode. Hardly anything is about the hour afterward, which is often the part that determines how the rest of your day goes, and the part people routinely handle badly by trying to pretend nothing happened.

Why you feel so wrung out

Going far away is not a restful state. Whatever you call it, spacing out or dissociating, your system has just been through something expensive: a surge of alarm, a protective turning down of the volume, and then the slow business of coming back up. The tiredness afterward is real and physical, the way it is real after a fright or a long cry.

The fog is part of coming back, not a sign that you are still gone. Attention and memory return at different speeds. For a while you may lose words, read the same line four times, feel slightly unreal around the edges, or find your limbs clumsy. That is the system reconnecting in order, and it is temporary.

What usually makes it worse is the second layer: the review. What is wrong with me. Did anyone notice. Why does this keep happening. I have ruined the afternoon. All of that is more alarm poured into a system that is trying to settle, and it reliably lengthens the fog.

Do the first ten minutes physically

Before you try to think or decide anything, treat this as a body problem for a few minutes.

Water. Actual water, a full glass. Something to eat with a bit of substance in it, because coming back often finds you empty. Cool air on your face. Feet on the floor. Shoulders dropped. A slow walk, even once around the room. Weight helps: a heavy blanket, your back against a wall, sitting down properly rather than perching.

If you can be warm and left alone for a few minutes, take that. If you cannot, take the smallest version of it: a bathroom, a stairwell, the car, two minutes in the fresh air.

Say where and when you are

Out loud, if it is private enough, or under your breath. I am in the hallway at work. It is Tuesday. It is October. That is over.

That last sentence matters. The state has a way of leaving a residue, a sense that whatever it was is still happening. Saying plainly that it has ended gives your system the information it is missing.

Lower the demands for an hour

This is the part people resist. The urge afterward is to prove the episode cost nothing by getting straight back to the hardest thing in front of you, which almost always produces a worse hour and a second episode later.

Instead, downgrade. Move the demanding task to tomorrow, or to this afternoon. Do something undemanding and absorbing: tidy a shelf, answer easy messages, walk, cook something simple. Avoid big decisions and difficult conversations for a while; you are a poor judge of both from this side of the fog, and almost nothing truly needs to be settled in the next hour.

Do not interrogate yourself yet

There is a useful conversation to have about triggers and patterns. The foggy hour is not when to have it. Looking hard at the episode while you are still coming back tends to produce frightening conclusions and very little insight.

Write two lines instead, just as a record: what was happening before, how long it lasted. Look at it later, when you can think. Over weeks, those two-line notes become the pattern that actually helps.

Expect an emotional tail

Often the feelings the episode was holding off arrive once you are back. Sadness, irritability, a wish to be held, a wish to be left completely alone, tearfulness over something small. This is not you falling apart. It is the volume coming back up, and it usually passes within a few hours.

Treat the aftermath as information

If an episode leaves you flattened for the rest of the day, that is worth noticing. It usually means your reserves were already low: sleep, food, weeks of pressure, something unresolved. The size of the aftermath says less about the episode than about what you were running on when it arrived.

Be reasonable with yourself about it

You did not choose this and you did not do it wrong. Something overwhelmed your system, your system protected you with the move it knows, and now you are tired. That is the whole story. Treat yourself roughly the way you would treat a friend who had just had a bad fright: warmth, something to drink, no questions for a while, fewer expectations for the afternoon.

When to get help

If the after-hours are becoming most of your week, if episodes are getting longer or more frequent, if you are losing time, or if it all started after something frightening, please bring it to your doctor, who can rule out physical causes, and to a therapist who works with trauma and anxiety. Coming back from an episode is a skill that gets better with practice, and it gets better faster with someone helping you build it.', array['Self-Compassion', 'Grounding']::text[], 0, '2026-10-20T15:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
  excerpt      = excluded.excerpt,
  body_md      = excluded.body_md,
  tags         = excluded.tags,
  min_level    = 0,
  published_at = excluded.published_at;
